<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.2: Modify Expressions to Translate Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02046</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.2: Modify Expressions to Translate Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f45dbcd2-cecc-40c4-88bf-c335390551a0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ad776e2f4b03a08bf69ddddc59a1efd31551b1a0">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
</div>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>How would you change the equation \(y=x^2\) so that the vertex of the graph of the new equation is located at the following coordinates and the graph opens as described?</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((0,11)\), opens up </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>\(y=x^2+11\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((7,11)\), opens up </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x−7)^2+11\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((7,-3)\), opens down </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-1(x−7)^2−3\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Verify your predictions. </para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4843161eef643f7108bb7fb8af0693b68859ca05"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Kiran graphed the equation \(y=x^2+1\) and noticed that the vertex is at \((0,1)\). He changed the equation to \(y=(x−3)^2+1\) and saw that the graph shifted 3 units to the right and the vertex is now at \((3,1)\).</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="ef8dbd65-b098-47e9-b311-e1ee015c1c45" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a6a19eb6-3012-4617-a5d6-28ceacee7df9" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Disagree" data-solution-options="[&quot;Agree&quot;, &quot;Disagree&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Do you agree with Kiran?  </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Disagree</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Explain or show your reasoning why you agree or disagree with Kiran. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>You should disagree with Kiran.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8257db4bc20f3131296727fde38411df9fe0e233"/></media></figure></para>
  <list>
    <item> The vertex of the graph of the original equation \(y=x^2+2x+1\) is at \((-1,0)\). The vertex of \(y=(x−5)^2+2x+1\) is at \((4,10)\). </item>
    <item> Evaluating \(y=(x−5)^2+2x+1\) at \(x = 4\) gives \(y=10\), not \(y=0\). </item>
    <item> The original equation is not in vertex form, so changing \(x^2\) into \((x−5)^2\) does not change the location of the vertex 5 units to the right. </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Q4a-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(y=(x+4)^2-1\) is a parabola with a vertex at \((-4,1)\) that opens upward.</para>
    <para> If we multiply \(y\) by 2, we get \(2y=2[(x+4)^2-1] = 2(x+4)^2-2\). How does this change the graph? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>The parabola is narrower and the vertex is lower. The \(x\)-intercepts stay the same.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--end question-->
<!--Q4b-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>If we multiply \(y\) by \(\frac{1}{2}\), we get \(y=\frac{1}{2}[(x+4)^2-1] =\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^2-\frac{1}{2}.\) How does this change the graph?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>The parabola is wider and the vertex is higher. The \(x\)-intercepts stay the same.
  </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--end question-->
<!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERACTIVE-->
<!--START QUESTION # 5-->
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Verify your prediction using Desmos tool below. </para>
    <para>After you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt="Graph of the equations y equals x plus four squared minus one, y equals two times x plus 4 squared minus two, and y equals twelve times x plus 4 squared minus twelve"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/faff41a5e75f54fcfc04529b6122df184030cb64"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><!--END ADD DESMOS INTERACTIVE-->
<!--Q6a-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Sophia graphed the equation \(y=x^2-6x+9\). She wondered what would happen to the graph if everywhere she had an \(x\), she replaced it with \(3x\). </para>
    <para> Sophia predicted that the parabola would be narrower, and the
        \(x\)-intercepts and \(y\)-intercepts would change. Write why you agree or disagree with Sophia’s prediction.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>The parabola will be narrower and the \(x\)-intercepts will change, but the \(y\)-intercept will stay the same.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--end question-->
<!--Q6b-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Sophia also wondered what would happen if she replaced the \(x\) in \(y=x^2-6x+9\) with \(\frac{1}{3}x\), how that would change the graph. She predicted that the parabola would be wider, and the \(x\)-intercepts and \(y\)-intercepts would change. Write why you agree or disagree with Sophia’s prediction.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>The parabola will be wider and the \(x\)-intercepts will change, but the \(y\)-intercept will stay the same.
  </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--end question-->
